*Abstraction

Definition:
Abstraction is the process of hiding implementation details and showing only essential features to the user.

In simple words:
What to do is shown, how to do is hidden.

Why Abstraction is needed

Reduces complexity
Improves security
Makes code easy to maintain
Supports loose coupling
How abstraction is achieved in Java

Java supports abstraction using:

Abstract class
Interface

1) Abstraction using Abstract Class
Rules

Abstract class is declared using abstract keyword

It can have:

Abstract methods (no body)
Normal methods (with body)
Object of abstract class cannot be created

Example
abstract class Vehicle {

    abstract void start();   // abstract method

    void fuel() {            // normal method
        System.out.println("Vehicle needs fuel");
    }
}

class Bike extends Vehicle {

    void start() {
        System.out.println("Bike starts with kick");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vehicle v = new Bike();
        v.start();
        v.fuel();
    }
}

2) Abstraction using Interface
Rules

Interface uses interface keyword

All methods are public and abstract by default

Supports multiple inheritance

Variables are public static final

Example
interface Bank {
    void rateOfInterest();
}

class SBI implements Bank {

    public void rateOfInterest() {
        System.out.println("SBI rate is 6.5%");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Bank b = new SBI();
        b.rateOfInterest();
    }
}

Real-life Example

ATM machine:

User sees only options (withdraw, deposit)

Internal process is hidden
→ This is abstraction

Difference: Abstract Class vs Interface
Feature	      Abstract Class	       Interface
1)Methods	 Abstract + Normal	      Only abstract
2)Variables	     Normal	           public static final
3)Multiple         No                     yes
inheritance
4)Keyword	    abstract	             interface